128 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 385 0 R
/E (1. What is Kubernetes, and how does it work?)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
129 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Kubernetes is an open-source container orchestration platform for automating deployment, scaling, and management of containerized applications. It works by managing containers across a cluster of nodes, ensuring high availability and scalability through its master and worker architecture.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
130 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 386 0 R
/E (2. Explain the difference between a Pod and a Container.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (A container is a lightweight unit that packages code and dependencies. A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes and can hold one or more containers sharing the same network namespace and storage.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 387 0 R
/E (3. What is a Namespace in Kubernetes, and why is it used?)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
133 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (A Namespace is a way to divide cluster resources between multiple users. It provides isolation and helps manage resources in large environments.)
